    {
      "id": "el01105",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 69.59,
      "y": 22595.0,
      "width": 1260.82,
      "height": 100.0,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "grp_diagram_18",
        "grp_column"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 35775319,
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 356626048,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1795621586000,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "\"Kafka EOS gives atomic consume-process-produce WITHIN Kafka. When the process step writes to an external database, the\ndatabase commit and the Kafka commit are two independent commits; a crash between them can duplicate or lose. So I run\nat-least-once and make the database write idempotent with a unique key, which gives an exactly-once EFFECT in Postgres. If I\nneed Kafka-to-Kafka stream processing, then transactions (or Kafka Streams with exactly_once_v2) are the right tool.\"",
      "originalText": "\"Kafka EOS gives atomic consume-process-produce WITHIN Kafka. When the process step writes to an external database, the database commit and the Kafka commit are two independent commits; a crash between them can duplicate or lose. So I run at-least-once and make the database write idempotent with a unique key, which gives an exactly-once EFFECT in Postgres. If I need Kafka-to-Kafka stream processing, then transactions (or Kafka Streams with exactly_once_v2) are the right tool.\"",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "containerId": "el01104",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "index": "c0Hr"
    },
